ContrAlto 2: make it survivable as a window-manager-less kiosk.

THREE edits, all required before the Xerox Alto tile can be captured or driven.
Applied by scripts/build-guests/tiles/alto.sh to a clean checkout of
jdersch/Contralto2 @ e3681fbc30d129172b4c306aaee8c4e71ae1a458.

1. KioskMode = True CRASHES upstream, on every platform, before the first frame:

     Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException: No parent window found.
        at ContraltoUI.ViewModels.ViewModelBase.FindWindowByViewModel(...)
        at ContraltoUI.ViewModels.AltoUIViewModel.set_FullScreenDisplay(Boolean)
        at ContraltoUI.ViewModels.AltoUIViewModel..ctor(...)

   The AltoUIViewModel constructor sets FullScreenDisplay = true when the config
   asks for kiosk mode, and the setter measures the screen through the window
   that the view model is not yet attached to. Guarding the lookup is enough:
   with no window there is no screen to measure, and the fallback scale of 1:1
   is exactly right for this exhibit, whose X root is the Alto's own bitmap.

   KioskMode is what hides the Avalonia menu bar and the Fields/Sec status bar
   (both bind to !FullScreenDisplay), so nothing else can keep emulator chrome
   out of the captured framebuffer.

2. WITH NO WINDOW MANAGER, NOTHING PLACES THE WINDOW. Avalonia leaves it at its
   default +10+10, which paints a black L down the left and top of the captured
   root and pushes 10 rows of the Alto's 808-line portrait picture off the
   bottom. Measured on the box, before this edit: "Contralto" 608x816+10+10 on a
   608x816 root. After: 608x808+0+0 on a 608x808 root, with no border, no menu
   and no letterbox. The window also asks for no decorations a WM would have to
   honour, so this is inert anywhere a WM does exist.

3. AND NOTHING FOCUSES IT EITHER, WHICH IS THE EXPENSIVE ONE. The Alto keyboard
   is wired to AltoDisplay.OnKeyDown/OnKeyUp, and AltoDisplay is a Focusable
   UserControl that in normal use receives Avalonia focus because a user CLICKS
   the display. Nothing clicks anything in a kiosk. Unpatched, X delivers every
   keystroke to the right window, the guest kernel's i8042 counter goes up, the
   emulator is running at 170% of a core — and the Alto never sees a key. The
   Executive sits there blinking its cursor, which is indistinguishable from a
   dead emulator, and the two obvious wrong diagnoses (a stalled emulator, a
   paused guest) each cost a build. Focusing the control on load fixes it.

   Note that X input focus is NOT the problem here and does not need XSetInputFocus:
   with no WM the focus is PointerRoot, and this window covers the whole root.
